{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'critical rationalism' that maximizes verisimilitude (truth-likeness) without assigning probability; however, accepting this defense costs the scientist any guarantee of predictive success, reducing rational action to a gamble on the theory that has best withstood falsification so far, rather than one likely to be true.","counterconditions":["If 'verisimilitude' cannot be formally defined or measured without relying on inductive assumptions about the structure of reality, the distinction collapses into disguised induction.","If the practical necessity of acting 'as if' the preferred theory will hold in the future functionally equates to believing it is probable, then the semantic separation is merely verbal and fails to escape Hume's problem.","If Popper's appeal to 'biological propensity' or 'psychological faith' to bridge the gap between corroboration and action is admitted, the system surrenders its claim to pure logical rationality."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'justification of truth' (which requires induction and is rejected) and 'rationality of choice' (which relies on the logical asymmetry of falsification). By choosing the theory that survives the severest tests, the scientist selects the one with the highest current verisimilitude content, not the highest probability. This avoids induction because past survival does not imply future survival; it only implies that the theory has not yet been shown false, making it the most informative and thus the most rational target for further criticism and tentative application.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}